    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The formation of neuronal synapses in the nematode Caenorhabditis elegans (C. elegans) is a highly regulated process dependent on the recruitment of synapse assembly proteins to the presynaptic active zone. Other proteins involved in the synaptogenic process, as well as the molecular mechanisms through which they act, remain unknown. Recent work has shown that essential genes play important roles in synaptogenesis (P. H. Chia, Li, & Shen, 2013; Firnhaber & Hammarlund, 2013). Previously, the role of essential genes in the C. elegans nervous system has been difficult to study due to lethality associated with loss of function mutations in functional domains of essential genes. However, recent advances in RNAi have allowed the generation of C. elegans strains that facilitate targeted neuron-specific RNAi.\r\nHere, we employ a tissue specific RNAi strategy to identify ztf-11, a zinc finger transcription factor, as an essential gene involved in synaptogenesis in the Hermaphrodite Specific Neuron (HSN). To determine the ZTF-11 expression pattern as well as its tissue specific requirements we inserted a conditional degradation motif (ZF1) fused to a C-terminal GFP tag via the CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing system. Using fluorescence microscopy we found that that ZTF-11::ZF1::GFP was highly expressed in neural progenitor cells and neurons in a highly regulated spatiotemporal fashion. Cell-specific degradation of ZTF-11 in HSN demonstrated that ZTF-11 is essential for proper synapse formation. Taken together, our study provides new mechanistic insights into the role of ztf-11 in synapse assembly through regulation of downstream genes that may directly influence assembly of active zone proteins and their functional roles in synaptogenesis and neurogenesis.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
